Route-Aware Immunotherapy Target Atlas for Desmoplastic Small Round Cell Tumor.  Abstract Background/Aim: Desmoplastic small round cell tumor (DSRCT) is an EWSR1::WT1-driven sarcoma with limited disease-specific options. We curated an extensive set of transcriptomic targets and reported bulk tumor RNA prevalence, with further organization of potential immunotherapy targets based on organ, cellular, subcellular and proteomic evidence. &nbsp;The result is a putative target atlas for DSRCT.&nbsp; 
 Materials and Methods: An 80-gene candidate-and-control panel was examined in two bulk RNA-sequencing cohorts comprising 61 patients and 86 tumor specimens. Technical replicate libraries were collapsed to specimens, &nbsp;and specimens to patients, &nbsp;on each cohort's native counts per million (CPM) or transcripts per million (TPM) scale; threshold and collapse-rule sensitivity analyses were performed. Single-cell data (one patient, two usable specimens), spatial data (five patients, six specimens), three fusion-knockdown cohorts, one cell-state cohort, and Human Protein Atlas (HPA)/Genotype-Tissue Expression (GTEx) normal-tissue references provided exploratory evidence about cellular source, fusion-associated regulation, cell state, and expression in normal tissues. 
 Results: CD276, CD200, ERBB2, ALCAM, IGF2, TFRC, and the established diagnostic marker CACNA2D2 were detected in 61/61 patients. CLDN6 was detected in 33/61 (54%; Wilson 95% confidence interval (CI)=42-66%) and SSTR3 in 59/61 (97%; 95% CI=89-99%). CACNA2D2, FGFR4, and MSLN expression decreased after EWSR1::WT1 suppression in all three cohorts, whereas results for several other candidates differed among cohorts.&nbsp; 
 Conclusion: The atlas identifies the next validation study required for each target; RNA detection alone does not establish protein abundance, therapeutic accessibility, efficacy, or safety.
